course: dictionary
lesson: 6620
title: Nostril: the openings in your nose
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A nostril is one of the two openings at the end of your nose. Air goes in and out through them, so you breathe and smell through your nostrils. «Seals can hold their breath for many minutes and close their nostrils tightly when they dive.» The clues are hold their breath and close: the nostrils are the openings that air goes through, and the seal shuts them to keep water out.
explain: Read the words around the word to find its meaning. In «The sloth bear closes its nostrils, blows away dust and sucks termites up through the gap», the bear shuts its nose openings so dust cannot get in. Ask: what word would fit here? Try nose openings in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Animals have nostrils too, but they are placed in different ways. «A crocodile's eyes, ears and nostrils are on top of its head» tells you the crocodile can breathe while the rest of its body is under water. Context shows you where the nostrils are, even when the word itself does not say it.
fact: A nostril is one of the two openings at the end of your nose.
fact: Two or more nostrils are called nostrils.
fact: Tiny hairs and mucus inside your nostrils trap dust and germs.
fact: «Seals close their nostrils tightly when they dive.»
fact: Never push small objects into your nostrils; ask a grown-up for help if something gets stuck.
exercise: «Seals close their nostrils tightly when they dive.» What is a nostril? | Sharp teeth used for hunting | *Openings at the end of the nose that air goes through | Small bones inside the ears | Large flat fins on the back
exercise: «The kiwi of New Zealand has nostrils at the tip of its beak.» Where are the kiwi's nostrils? | On its wings | *At the tip of its beak | Under its feet | On its back
exercise: Why should you never push small objects into your nostrils? | To help you smell better | To make a funny noise | *Because they could get stuck and hurt you | To see how far they go
test: «The sloth bear closes its nostrils and blows away dust.» What does the bear do to keep dust out? | Blows bubbles | Covers its eyes | *Shuts its nostrils | Hides in a tree
test: What do we call two or more nostrils? | Nostrilly | *Nostrils | Nosetrils | Nostril's
exam: Why do seals close their nostrils when they dive? | To smell fish better | To sing louder | *To keep water out | To warm their noses
